 ’24, New York, NY, USA, pp. 33–39. External Links: ISBN 9798400704185, 【99†Link†doi.org】, 【100†Document†dx.doi.org】 Cited by: 【86†§2.1】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZK-Flex: A Flexible and Scalable Framework for Accelerating Zero-Knowledge Proofs (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.03046v1)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view1\",\"pattern\":\"memory\"}); Total lines: 234\nL122: We further apply three optimizations: (1) signed-digit representation to reduce bucket count without increasing windows (【47†Xavier, 2022】), (2) parallel reverse prefix sum for bucket aggregation (【48†Liu et al., 2024】), and (3) mixed point addition (MPADD), which operates on affine–Jacobian pairs and reduces modular multiplications (【46†Housni and Botrel, 2022】), enabling more parallel engines. However, simply increasing the number of MPADD engines does not guarantee full utilization.\nL123: As discussed previously, in the conventional window-level parallelism strategy, each MPADD engine processes a distinct window and requires its own bucket memory set to avoid memory conflicts. If sufficient bucket memory sets are unavailable, the fully unrolled window-level strategy cannot be applied, forcing multiple MPADD engines to share the same window.\nL124: Figure 7. (a) Linked-list memory chains point addresses within each bucket. (b) Bucket assigner maps buckets to MPADD engines.\nL125: Linked-list memory management for enhanced parallelism under limited memory space. To address memory constraints, we introduce a linked-list memory technique (Fig. 【62†7】) that clusters points in each bucket via pointers, enabling dynamic allocation to MPADD engines with conflict-free access to bucket and point memories. The linked-list memory is populated on the fly as scalar information is written to the chip, thereby hiding the associated latency.\nL126: This method increases the scalar memory requirement by roughly two times compared to the standard design, but the scalar memory footprint remains negligible compared to the bucket memory. The implementation requires two small memories: (1) a head memory that stores the initial pointers for each bucket, and (2) a link memory where each entry records the next point address and its corresponding linked list pointer.\nL127: Finally, a bucket assigner distributes buckets across MPADD engines to ensure conflict free updates within a shared window. By adopting this technique, we enable highly parallel MPADD operations while preventing the superlinear growth of bucket memory as parallelism increases.\nL128: ## 4. Evaluation\nL129: ### 4.1. Hardware Modeling\nL130: For the hardware model of ZK-Flex, we implement the PEs, shared memories, TCores, and interconnects in SystemVerilog and synthesize them using a commercial 28 nm PDK, targeting minimal area and power at a 1 GHz frequency. ZK-Flex employs single-port, multi-bank, 128-bit, 1 GHz SRAMs for the on-chip memory nodes, providing 34 MB of total capacity. Each TCore also includes a 2.13 KB local shared memory using the same SRAM organization.\nL131: TCore–TCore links use 768-bit ports at 1 GHz, yielding 4.608 TB/s of bisection bandwidth across the full-duplex $8\\times 8$ ruche network. Table 【63†1】 summarizes the synthesis results for a configuration with 36 TCore and 28 memory-node instances. The complete chip occupies 205.512 mm^{2} and consumes up to 48.520 W at 1 GHz.\nL132: Table 1. Area and power breakdown of ZK-Flex.\nL133: Component  | Area (mm^{2})  | Power (W)\nL134: 45$\\times$ Eval. PE Group  | 0.023  | 0.0099\nL135: 45$\\times$ Int. PE Group.  | 1.507  | 0.7488\nL136: 45$\\times$ Interp. PE Group.  | 0.137  | 0.0594\nL137: Hier. Adder Tree  | 0.251  | 0.1090\nL138: TCore Ctrl.  | 0.004  | 0.0096\nL139: 24$\\times$ ModAdder (128b)  | 0.069  | 0.0240\nL140: Shared Mem. (2.13 KB)  | 0.0065  | 0.0006\nL141: TCore subtotal  | 1.998  | 0.961\nL